{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Y1moVDz8PHGuohzsZgQJQn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project State Analysis\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as test beds. The goal is to derive formal specs from C source, generate Go implementations using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of utilities (one package per utility)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, human-readable sizes)\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Mage build targets\n- `docs/specs/` - PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current State**: Code exists at generation-merged tags but not on the working branch, with rel01.0 specs in progress and later releases pending. No Go code is currently on the working branch, and there's an open issue to implement the differential testing harness for the first release.\n\n**Current Release**: rel01.0 - The first batch focuses on the TypeScript specification and differential testing, with only one use case in progress and code not yet started. The blocking dependency is implementing `pkg/testutils`, which is tracked as the first open issue.\n\n**Dependency Chain**: Libraries need to come before CLI tools, and foundational code before features. For this release, I need `pkg/testutils` (the differential testing harness) first, then `cmd/ts` (the TypeScript implementation). The existing issue already covers the testutils work, so the next logical step would be proposing the `cmd/ts` implementation—but I'm constrained to one task, and the testutils issue is already in flight. I see the existing pkg/testutils issue from a previous session is still open, so I shouldn't duplicate it. Since I can only propose one task and pkg/testutils is the blocking dependency, the next logical step would be cmd/ts once that's completed. But I'm realizing the constraint here is about what to propose in this planning pass—the existing issue represents work already tracked, so I should focus on what comes next in the sequence. stitch agent will implement code. So I should propose tasks for the stitch agent to implement.\n\nSince `pkg/testutils` is already an open issue (tracked), I should propose the implementation of `cmd/ts` as the next task... but it depends on pkg/testutils being done first.\n\nHmm, but actually, looking at this more carefully - the \"issues\" in this context are the tasks for the stitch agent. The issue `generation-2026-02-26-23-16-32-dtl` is an open task for the stitch agent to implement pkg/testutils. \n\nSo my job is to propose the next task. With only 1 task allowed, and pkg/testutils already being tracked, I should:\n1. Not duplicate pkg/testutils (already tracked)\n2. Propose cmd/ts as the next task (with dependency on pkg/testutils)\n\nThe dependency field only works for within-batch dependencies, so I can't reference pkg/testutils by index. I'll set dependency to -1 and note the dependency in required_reading instead, letting the orchestrator handle the execution order.\n\nFor cmd/ts, I need to cover R1-R8 (implementation) and R9 (differential testing). Per the planning constitution, I should split implementation and tests into separate tasks, but I only have 1 task slot. So I'll propose cmd/ts implementation covering R1-R8, and note that R9 (differential testing) should be a follow-up task.\n\nSince cmd/ts is a command binary without separate type definitions, I can combine types and implementation into one task. I'll structure this as implementing the core timestamp utility with all its modes and features. I'm looking at the design decisions and acceptance criteria from the PRD—I need to use Go's built-in monotonic clock via time.Now(), handle subsecond precision with microseconds, manage timezone handling for elapsed modes, and read stdin line-by-line with bufio.Scanner. The acceptance criteria confirm I need to validate basic functionality like timestamped output, monotonically increasing elapsed times, proper error handling for invalid flags, and clean compilation.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the task scope—there's already an open issue for pkg/testutils, and I can only propose one new task given the constraint. Since implementation should come before tests per the guidelines, I'll propose cmd/ts implementation as the next task rather than splitting it into separate implementation and test tasks.\n\nLooking at the line count, the original Perl source is around 400 lines with comments, so a Go implementation covering flag parsing, the main loop for reading stdin, all the different format modes, regex matching, relative time conversion, and exit code handling should fit comfortably within the 250-line target at roughly 200-300 lines.\n\nWriting the task description now... Looking at the requirements for the ts binary, I need to handle the custom format extensions for subsecond precision (%.S, %.s, %.T), implement the incremental mode with the -i flag to track elapsed time between lines, and add the elapsed-since-start mode with the -s flag that both use GMT timezone by default and are mutually exclusive with each other.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining modes: the -m flag for monotonic clock readings that work across all format options, the -r flag for scanning and converting recognized timestamp patterns (syslog, ISO-8601, RFC 2822, lastlog formats) into relative age strings like \"15m5s ago\", and ensuring lines without matching timestamps pass through unchanged.\n\nI also need to handle the exit codes properly—clean exit on EOF, non-zero with usage to stderr for bad flags, and graceful SIGPIPE handling. The TZ environment variable should be respected for wall-clock timestamps in default mode, but internally forced to GMT for elapsed time calculations in -i and -s modes.\n\nFor the implementation approach, I'm planning to use time.Now() for all clock reads since Go's time package automatically includes monotonic readings. time.Time values, satisfying -m mode without special clock selection. For strftime, I'll map standard directives to Go's time.Format layout strings, handling subsecond extensions by formatting the base component and appending fractional seconds from nanoseconds. For -i and -s elapsed formatting, I'll compute the time delta, convert to seconds as an integer, then use Unix time formatting under GMT semantics to match the Perl source. Rather than bufio.Scanner which can drop content exceeding its buffer, I'll use bufio.NewReader with ReadString to handle partial last lines correctly. For -r mode, I'm setting up regex patterns at package init to match the four timestamp formats from the spec, then computing relative age with time.Since and formatting as a human-readable string like '15m5s ago' using integer minutes and seconds.\n\nThe acceptance criteria cover the core behaviors: basic timestamp output matching the expected format, monotonically increasing elapsed times with -s, proper error handling for invalid flags and conflicting options, graceful handling of empty input, and relative timestamp replacement in -r mode.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the scope—this is substantial functionality across multiple modes and flag combinations. The -r mode alone requires regex matching against four different timestamp patterns and computing relative ages, which could easily be 50-80 lines. The full implementation might stretch to 250-350 lines total, which exceeds the 150-250 line target for a single task. I should probably split this into smaller, more focused tasks. I'm weighing whether to split the cmd/ts implementation further, but since I can only create one task in this session, I need to pick the most fundamental piece. The core modes are more essential than the relative-time conversion, so I'll propose the full implementation targeting 150-200 lines, which should fit comfortably within the 250-line constraint based on the Perl reference implementation.\n\nNow I'm checking the structural details: the file should be `cmd/ts/main.go` with `package main`, and since there's already a go.mod at the project root defining the module path, I don't need to create a separate one. The key is anchoring the requirements to the PRD R-numbers and making sure the acceptance criteria are verifiable against those specifications. I'm looking at the requirements for the ts timestamp utility. Beyond the default behavior of prepending timestamps to stdin, I need to support custom format strings with strftime conversions and ts-specific subsecond extensions like %.S for seconds.microseconds, plus an incremental mode with the -i flag that shows elapsed time between lines. I need to implement a relative-time conversion mode with the -r flag that scans lines for various timestamp patterns like syslog, ISO-8601, RFC 2822, and lastlog formats, then replaces them with relative age strings; if a custom format is provided, it converts to strftime output instead, and this mode is mutually exclusive with -i and -s. Then I'm moving into exit codes and error handling requirements. For the subsecond extensions in strftime, I'll extract the base component and append the microsecond portion using Nanosecond(). For elapsed timestamps in -i and -s modes, I'm converting the duration to seconds and formatting it as UTC to match the Perl source behavior. I'm using bufio.NewReader with ReadString to handle stdin correctly, including partial last lines without the Scanner's buffer limitations.\n\nFor -r mode, I'll compile the timestamp regexes once at startup and calculate relative age using time.Since(), then format it as a human-readable duration string like \"5m30s ago\" or \"2h15m45s ago\" depending on the length.\n\nI also need to handle several edge cases: unrecognized flags should print usage to stderr and exit non-zero, empty stdin should produce no output and exit cleanly, conflicting flags like -i and -s together should error out, and the -r mode should replace absolute timestamps with relative age strings in the output.\n\nNow I'm estimating the line count for this implementation—accounting for package setup, flag parsing with getopt-style handling for -i, -s, -m, and -r options, a strftime translation function to map common directives, subsecond extension support, the main read loop, elapsed time formatting logic, regex-based replacement for -r mode, and a SIGPIPE handler. This should come to around 205 lines, which fits comfortably within the 150-250 target range.\n\nAll the design decisions trace back to specific requirements from the PRD—the strftime translation from R2.3, subsecond handling from R3.2 and R4.2, line reading from R1.1, and the other features from their respective requirements. I should include go.mod in the required_reading since the stitch agent needs the module path for the package declaration, though no external dependencies are needed since this uses only the standard library.\n\nNow I'm verifying the issue structure has everything: the deliverable type, required reading, the single file, eight requirements, five design decisions, and seven acceptance criteria all check out.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73958,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73958},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d070147e-fe11-42ab-9060-f5c4a942012f","uuid":"78e9bdf7-a487-4285-8d9e-797080adeedc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Y1moVDz8PHGuohzsZgQJQn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils derives formal specs from C source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations via an AI coding agent, and verifies behavioral parity through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries. The goal is to find where the methodology breaks down.\n\n**Architecture**: Four stages per utility — extract (C source), specify (PRD + use case), synthesize (orchestrator generates Go), verify (differential testing). Shared libraries: `pkg/testutils` (harness), `pkg/sys` (syscalls), `pkg/format` (output formatting). Each utility lives in `cmd/<utility>/`.\n\n**Current state**: No Go code on the working branch (all generated code lives at generation-merged tags). Specs for rel01.0 are in progress. One open implementation issue exists: `generation-2026-02-26-23-16-32-dtl` tracks `pkg/testutils` (prd001 R1-R5).\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 — ts specification and differential test. One use case: `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (in_progress). Code not started.\n\n**Priority reasoning**: `pkg/testutils` is already tracked. The next dependency in canonical order (libraries before consumers, P5/P8) is `cmd/ts` — the ts binary itself. It depends only on the Go standard library and will use `pkg/testutils` for differential tests (which is a separate test task). With 1 task allowed, the implementation comes before the test task.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/ts timestamp utility (prd004-ts R1-R8)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd004-ts.yaml\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component, project structure)\n      - go.mod (module path: github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ts/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: ts binary -- read stdin line by line and prepend strftime timestamps\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default behavior (prd004-ts R1.1-R1.6): read stdin line by line with bufio.NewReader ReadString, prepend a timestamp formatted with '%b %d %H:%M:%S' and a space before each line, flush stdout after every line, pass through partial last lines without trailing newline, and exit 0 on clean EOF.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement custom format string (prd004-ts R2.1-R2.4): accept an optional positional argument as a strftime format string; translate standard strftime directives to Go time.Format layout strings; implement ts-specific subsecond extensions %.S (seconds.microseconds), %.s (unix-epoch.microseconds), %.T (HH:MM:SS.microseconds) by formatting the base component then appending '.' and six decimal digits from a single time.Now() call per line.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement incremental mode (prd004-ts R3.1-R3.4): -i flag timestamps each line with elapsed time since the previous line (first line uses elapsed since start); default format '%H:%M:%S' with UTC for elapsed formatting; -i and -s are mutually exclusive -- print usage error to stderr and exit non-zero if both are given.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement elapsed-since-start mode (prd004-ts R4.1-R4.3): -s flag timestamps each line with total elapsed time since ts started, monotonically increasing; default format '%H:%M:%S' with UTC for elapsed formatting; overridden by a custom format argument.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement monotonic clock mode (prd004-ts R5.1-R5.3): -m flag selects time.Now() monotonic reading for all timestamp samples; compatible with all format modes including subsecond extensions; timestamps must not jump backwards.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement relative-time conversion mode (prd004-ts R6.1-R6.5): -r flag scans each line for recognized timestamp patterns -- syslog ('Jan  5 14:30:00'), ISO-8601 ('2024-01-05T14:30:00.000Z'), RFC 2822 with optional day, lastlog ('Mon Jan  5 14:30') -- and replaces each match with a relative age string (e.g., '15m5s ago'); if a custom format argument is also given, reformat matched timestamps via that strftime format instead; lines without a recognized timestamp pass through unchanged; -r is mutually exclusive with -i and -s.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement exit codes and error handling (prd004-ts R7.1-R7.3): exit 0 on clean stdin EOF; exit non-zero and print a usage message to stderr for unrecognized flags; handle SIGPIPE by installing a signal handler that calls os.Exit(0) when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer, suppressing broken-pipe errors.\"\n      - id: R8\n        text: \"Implement TZ environment handling (prd004-ts R8.1-R8.2): honor the TZ environment variable for wall-clock timestamps in default mode by loading the time.Location from the TZ value before formatting; use UTC internally for elapsed timestamp formatting in -i and -s modes regardless of the process TZ setting, matching the TZ=GMT behavior of the Perl source.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use time.Now() for all clock reads. Go's time.Time automatically carries a monotonic reading, so -m mode requires no special clock selection -- the same call satisfies both wall-clock and monotonic requirements (prd004-ts R5.1).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Implement strftime by translating standard directives (%Y, %m, %d, %H, %M, %S, %b, %j, %p, %Z, %%, etc.) to Go time.Format layout strings before calling Format(). For the three ts subsecond extensions (%.S, %.s, %.T), extract the base component via Format(), then append '.' and fmt.Sprintf('%06d', t.Nanosecond()/1000) for microsecond precision (prd004-ts R2.3).\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For -i and -s elapsed timestamps, compute the duration, convert to whole seconds (int64), and call time.Unix(seconds, 0).UTC().Format(layout) to produce the HH:MM:SS string under UTC semantics, matching the TZ=GMT behavior of the Perl source (prd004-ts R3.2, R4.2).\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Use bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin) with ReadString('\\\\n') to read lines. This correctly handles partial last lines (no trailing newline at EOF) and avoids bufio.Scanner's 64 KB default buffer limit (prd004-ts R1.5).\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"For -r mode, compile the four timestamp regexes once at startup with regexp.MustCompile. Compute relative age as time.Since(parsedTime) and format as '<Xh><Ym><Zs> ago' with only non-zero components shown, except always show seconds (prd004-ts R6.1-R6.2).\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"echo 'hello world' | go run ./cmd/ts produces a line matching '<month> <day> <HH:MM:SS> hello world' using the default '%b %d %H:%M:%S' format (prd004-ts AC1).\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/ts -s produces three lines with monotonically non-decreasing elapsed timestamps in HH:MM:SS format (prd004-ts AC2).\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/ts -z (unrecognized flag) prints a usage message to stderr and exits with a non-zero code (prd004-ts AC4).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/ts with empty stdin (immediate EOF) produces no output and exits 0 (prd004-ts AC5).\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/ts -i -s prints a usage error to stderr and exits non-zero (prd004-ts R3.4).\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/ts -r on input 'Jan  1 00:00:00 boot message' outputs a line where the syslog timestamp is replaced by a relative age string followed by the remaining content (prd004-ts R6.1-R6.2).\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"cmd/ts compiles successfully with go build ./cmd/ts/... and produces no warnings under golangci-lint (prd004-ts AC6).\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73958,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73958},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d070147e-fe11-42ab-9060-f5c4a942012f","uuid":"387bf34c-af29-480b-a31a-801341e5228c"}
